    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0032",
      "sequence": 887,
      "meaning": "When Rāvaṇa reviles Rāma, Aṅgada becomes furious: hearing abuse of Hari or Hara brings sin equal to cow-slaughter. The elephant among monkeys roars and strikes the ground with both arms. Earth shakes, courtiers fall and flee as if seized by wind; Rāvaṇa tumbles and his crowns fall. He replaces some, while Aṅgada hurls four toward Rāma. Seeing them fly in daylight, monkeys fear an omen, but Rāma explains they are no comet or thunderbolt—only Rāvaṇa’s crowns, sent by Bali’s son. Hanumān catches them and lays them before him. Rāvaṇa orders Aṅgada seized and killed; the envoy smiles.",
