    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-lanka-kanda-0035",
      "sequence": 890,
      "meaning": "When all lose heart at Aṅgada’s strength, Rāvaṇa himself rises to seize the foot. Bali’s son tells him that grasping Aṅgada’s feet cannot save him—he must grasp Rāma’s. Ashamed, Rāvaṇa turns back, stripped of splendour like the midday moon, and sits as if all wealth were lost. How can one opposed to Rāma, soul and life of the world, find rest? His brow creates and destroys the cosmos, turns grass to thunderbolt and thunderbolt to grass; how could his envoy’s pledge fail? Aṅgada again teaches policy, but Time is near. Crushing enemy pride, he departs praising the Lord, telling Rāvaṇa that he spares him as a toy not worth killing. He returns thrilled and tearful to Rāma, while fearful demons brood and Mandodarī counsels again.",
